  "live_records": {
    "q-112077": {
      "dom_id": "step-q112077",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14242840/assets/cac1263052200f4794e983bd11236175646d4c808b5f8fee81ccfb58de12c5cf.png)\n\nWhich of the above could be the graph of $y=4{x}^{4}+x-1?$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's call $P(x)$ the given polynomial:\n\n$$\nP(x)=4{x}^{4}+x-1\n$$\n\nThe degree $(n=4)$ is even and the leading coefficient $({a}_{4}=4)$ is positive. Therefore, the polynomial has the following end behavior:\n\n- $P(x)\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to -\\infty .$\n\n- $P(x)\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nOnly the graph $B$ satisfies these conditions.",
