      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14242840/assets/43e2c01d6e2ee22fe666bbaa46586b43467f8dd580d76e4da6644ac845761d31.png)\n\nWhich of the above could be the graph of $y={x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-1?$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's call $P(x)$ the given polynomial:\n\n$$\nP(x)=2{x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}+8\n$$\n\nThe degree ($n=3$) is odd and the leading coefficient (${a}_{3}=1$) is positive. Therefore, the polynomial has the following end behavior:\n\n- $P(x)\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to -\\infty .$\n\n- $P(x)\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nOnly the graph $A$ satisfies these conditions.",
