    "q-38622": {
      "dom_id": "step-q38622",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14242840/assets/3d226fbaa993448544cb821d64843c6f2c11945f6ad869636e0c875ada530c72.png)\n\nWhich of the following polynomials could be the function shown in the diagram above?",
      "worked_solution": "Let's call $P(x)$ the polynomial shown in the diagram.\n\nThe end behavior of $P(x)$ is\n\n- $P(x)\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to -\\infty ,$ and\n\n- $P(x)\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nConsequently, \n\n- the degree of $P(x)$ is odd, and\n\n- the leading coefficient of $P(x)$ is positive.\n\n- the $y$-intercept is positive, which means that  $P(0)>0.$\n\nTherefore, of the given options, the only one that satisfies all three criteria is $y={x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+x+2.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
